course: everyday
lesson: 231
title: Campfires, cold nights and rivers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A campfire is lovely but it is real fire. You stay outside a safe circle round it, keep burning sticks in it, and never light one when there is a fire ban, because in dry weather one spark can start a wildfire.
explain: A fire is not out just because the ashes look grey. Embers can stay hot under the ash for many hours, so a fire is drowned with water, stirred and drowned again until it is cold to the touch.
explain: Rivers are trickier than they look. A calm surface can hide fast water underneath, banks can crumble, and after heavy rain a river turns into fast brown floodwater carrying logs.
explain: Example: in Victoria, Australia, a family on a cold camping night sleeps in hats and dry layers on foam mats, because the cold ground pulls heat out of a body much faster than the air does.
fact: On a cold night, sleep in a hat and dry layers on a mat, because the ground draws heat from your body.
fact: When there is a fire ban, do not light any fire at all, because one spark can start a wildfire.
fact: Stay about three big steps away from a campfire and never run around it, because you could trip and fall into the fire.
fact: Toast marshmallows on a long stick at the side of the flames, and blow one out if it catches fire, because burning sugar sticks to skin.
fact: Put a fire out by drowning it with water, stirring, and drowning again until it is cold to the touch.
fact: Hidden embers can stay hot for many hours and start a wildfire.
fact: Cool a burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell a grown-up; never put ice or butter on it.
fact: Keep burning sticks in the fire and never wave them around.
fact: Stay back from river banks, especially after rain, because they can be slippery or crumble.
fact: Water under a calm surface can still be moving fast enough to pull a swimmer downstream.
fact: Cross a river only on a bridge or with a grown-up who says it is safe, because wet stones and logs are slippery.
fact: Keep well away from a high brown river after heavy rain, because floodwater is fast and carries logs.
fact: Cross on the bridge, not on its railings, and never jump off a bridge into water, because hidden rocks or shallow water cause serious injuries.
exercise: The night in the tent is colder than expected. What helps most? | A hat, dry layers and a mat under you | Sleeping straight on the ground | Taking off layers so you shiver yourself warm | Sleeping in the clothes you got wet in
exercise: Why should you sleep on a mat and not straight on the ground? | The ground draws heat from your body | Mats keep all the insects out of the tent at night | The ground is too hard for the tent | Mats make the whole tent waterproof from underneath
exercise: There is a fire ban at the campsite. Can you light a tiny fire to toast marshmallows? | No, no fire at all may be lit | Yes, if it is very small | Yes, if a grown-up watches | Yes, at night when it is cooler
exercise: Why are fire bans made in dry weather? | One spark can start a wildfire | Smoke is bad for the trees and birds | Firewood is too expensive in summer | Fires are too hot in summer to sit near
exercise: How close should you sit to the campfire? | About three big steps away | Close enough to touch the stones | Right next to it to keep warm | It does not matter if you are careful
exercise: Your marshmallow catches fire. What should you do? | Blow it out, keeping the stick pointing away | Wave the stick round fast in the air to put it out | Grab it quickly with your fingers and squeeze it out | Put it straight in your mouth to stop the flame quickly
exercise: The campfire ashes look grey and cold. What should you do before leaving? | Drown it, stir and drown again until cold to the touch | Leave it, because grey ashes show the fire is clearly out | Cover it with dry leaves so nobody can see it | Kick a little sand over half of it
exercise: Why can a fire that looks out still be dangerous? | Hidden embers can stay hot for hours | Ashes turn back into flames by themselves at night | Grey ash is poisonous to animals | Firewood always relights in the morning
exercise: You touch a hot stone by the fire and burn your finger. What should you do? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell a grown-up | Put ice on it straight away and hold it there hard for as long as you can | Rub a big lump of butter on it | Wrap it up tightly in a bandage and keep going as if nothing happened
exercise: Your cousin pulls a burning stick out of the fire and waves it around. What should you tell him? | Keep it in the fire, as sparks can burn people | It is fine as long as he waves it slowly and carefully | Only wave it high above his head | Swing it round fast to make the flame go out faster
exercise: You want to play at the edge of the river after a night of rain. What should you do? | Stay well back from the edge | Stand right on the edge to look in | Climb down the bank to the water | Dangle your feet in from the bank
exercise: The river looks calm and still. Is it safe to swim across? | Not necessarily; water under the surface can be moving fast | Yes, calm water is never dangerous, even in a big river | Yes, as long as you are a strong swimmer who has had lessons | Yes, if you can see the bottom
exercise: Your friend wants to cross the river on slippery stepping stones. What should you do? | Use a bridge or ask a grown-up first | Go first and test the stones | Run across so you do not slip | Cross holding hands with your friend
exercise: After a storm the river is high, fast and brown. What should you do? | Keep well away from it | Go and watch from the edge of the bank | Throw sticks in to see how fast it is | Paddle at the edge where it is shallow
exercise: Older kids are jumping off a bridge into the river. Why should you not join them? | The water may be shallow or hide rocks | Jumping in makes the river water far too cold | The bridge will fall down if too many people jump | It is only dangerous on cold and rainy days
exercise: You want to see over the edge of a bridge. Where should you be? | On the bridge, behind the railings | Standing up on top of the railings | Sitting on top of the wall with your legs over | Hanging over the side
test: A girl in California is told there is a «total fire ban» but her uncle says a small fire will be fine. Which is true? | Any fire is forbidden, as one spark can start a wildfire | Small fires are always allowed during a ban, as long as a grown-up lights them | Fire bans are only for big forests | It is fine if they put it out properly afterwards with water
test: A camper pours a bit of water on the fire and goes to bed. Next morning, smoke is rising from the ashes. What should he have done? | Drowned, stirred and drowned again until cold | Left more wood on it so it could burn down slowly | Covered it with a blanket | Nothing, because a bit of smoke in the morning is normal
test: A boy burns his hand on a hot marshmallow. His friend fetches ice. What is better? | Cool running water for 20 minutes | Ice held on hard for a whole hour | Butter spread thickly over it | Toothpaste rubbed in
test: Children are playing tag around the campfire. What is the danger? | Someone can trip and fall into the fire | The fire will get bigger from the wind they make | The smoke will follow them round the camp | The marshmallows will burn while nobody is watching them
test: A boy in Wales sees a river with a smooth surface and thinks it must be slow. What is the truth? | It may still be fast and strong underneath | Smooth water is always slow, all the way down | Smooth water means the river is shallow there | Only the noisy, splashing rivers are ever dangerous
test: Why is a fallen log across a river a bad bridge? | It is wet and slippery, and a fall into the river is dangerous | It is far too wide for children to walk on | Logs float away the moment you step on them | Fish live under logs and bite at the feet of anyone crossing
test: A girl's sleeping bag is warm but she still feels cold from underneath. What would help most? | Putting a mat under her sleeping bag | Taking off her woolly hat | Opening the tent door to let fresh air in | Lying with her head outside the bag
test: After days of rain, some boys want to watch the floodwater from the river bank. Why is that unsafe? | The bank can crumble and floodwater is very fast | Floodwater is far too cold to stand and look at | The noise will frighten the fish away | It is only unsafe at night, when it is too dark to see the water
test: fire ban on but we just want marshmallows. small fire ok? | No fire at all during a ban | Yes, small fires don't count | Yes, if it's out by bedtime | Only if it's far from the tent
test: burnt my finger on the pan, put ice on it yeah? | No, cool running water for 20 minutes | Yes, ice is best for burns, hold it on | Rub butter on it | Wrap it up tight and just leave it
test: we're leaving camp, fire looks out. done? | No, drown it, stir and drown again till it's cold | Yes, if there's no flame left it's out | Kick dirt on it and go | Leave it, it will go out by itself in a bit
test: river looks dead calm, can i swim across | No, calm water can still pull you away | Yes, calm means no current at all | Yes, if you swim fast enough to the other side | Only if you can see the bottom
explain: A boy in Montana jumped across a fast river on wet stones; he slipped, the current pulled him under and his dad only just reached him.
explain: A camper in Australia poured petrol on a slow campfire; the flames burst up into his face and he was badly burned.
fact: Fast rivers are much stronger than they look, and even shallow fast water can knock a person over.
fact: Petrol and other fuel on a fire make it flare up in a flash and cause terrible burns.
test: river looks shallow, can i wade across | No, fast water can knock you over even if shallow | Yes, if it's only knee-deep it can't hurt u | Yes, run across | Only if you're holding a big stick to lean on
test: Why must you never pour fuel on a campfire? | The flames can burst up and burn you | It puts the fire out | It makes too much smoke | It wastes the fuel
test: fire's still glowing, we're heading off | Drown it with water and stir till it's cold | Leave it, it'll go out by itself soon | Cover it with leaves | Kick a bit of dirt on it n go, that's enough
test: did u hear some guy poured petrol on a campfire to get it going and it went WHOOSH and burnt his eyebrows off? real lesson? | Never put petrol or fuel on a fire | Use only a little petrol | Stand back a step when you pour it | Petrol is fine on small fires
test: apparently the river looked totally calm but a kid swimming got pulled away round the bend, they found him miles down. what's the true lesson? | Calm-looking water can still move fast | Calm rivers are always safe to swim | Only rough water is dangerous | Swimming against it always works
test: my cousin said a campfire from last week was still hot and set the whole forest on fire. probably exaggerated? | A week is a stretch, but hidden embers stay hot for hours | Fires go out on their own straight away | Embers are never hot after dark | Forests can't burn from small fires
exam: Identify the correct method of extinguishing a campfire. | Drowning, stirring and drowning again until it is cold | Covering it lightly with dry leaves | Leaving it to burn out by itself overnight while all sleep | Blowing upon it steadily until all the flames have vanished
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a fire ban? | No fire whatsoever is to be lit, as a spark may start a wildfire | Small fires are permitted during the hours of daylight | Fires are permitted provided that an adult attends them at all times | Fires are permitted near water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning shallow fast-flowing water. | It may knock a person over despite its shallow depth | It is always safe to wade across | It is dangerous only where it rises above the knee | It slows down considerably wherever it passes near stones
exam: Ascertain the reason for which one does not jump from a bridge into water. | Hidden rocks or shallow water may cause serious injuries | Bridges are too low for diving | The water is always too warm | The jump is too short to be enjoyable
